import java.sql.*;

public class SecureDatabaseConnection {
    public void fetchData() {
        String url = "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/myDatabase";
        String user = "root";
        String password = "";

        try {
            Class.forName("sun.jdbc.odbc.JdbcOdbcDriver");
            Connection con = DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, password);
            PreparedStatement pstmt = con.prepareStatement("SELECT * FROM users");

            // Using parameterized query to prevent SQL injection
            ResultSet rs = pstmt.executeQuery();

            while (rs.next()) {
                String username = rs.getString("username");
                String hashedPassword = rs.getString("password"); // In a real-world scenario, you'd want to compare hashed passwords

                System.out.println(username + " " + hashedPassword);
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

In the bad code snippet, the application is vulnerable to SQL injection attacks because it directly uses user-input (from the database) in the SQL query. This can be exploited by an attacker to manipulate the SQL query and gain unauthorized access to the data.

In the good code snippet, the application uses a parameterized query (PreparedStatement) to prevent SQL injection attacks. This ensures that user-input is treated as data and not executable SQL code, thus mitigating the risk of SQL injection.

